{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'python3 /workspace/tools.py read paper:din --find normalization --lines 22'","aggregated_output":"/bin/bash: warning: setlocale: LC_ALL: cannot change locale (C.UTF-8)\n/bin/bash: warning: setlocale: LC_ALL: cannot change locale (C.UTF-8)\n{\n  \"id\": \"paper:din\",\n  \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.06978v4\",\n  \"version\": \"content-sha256:5fee25f33f375e51b3ee9417d376ff9dbe2ea3628c932a7679ddfeb0feaaade2\",\n  \"access_scope\": \"archived exact-version primary text; caching does not establish a local mechanism result\",\n  \"text_sha256\": \"83fb9eeca31e2fa3c557b6f08b893ad0018cefb33da6e00952136b9ce17e607e\",\n  \"total_lines\": 831,\n  \"start_line\": 271,\n  \"next_line\": 293,\n  \"truncated\": false,\n  \"text\": \"271: et al., 2015).\\n272: However different from traditional attention method, the constraint of ∑iwi=1\\\\sum_{i}w_{i}=1 is relaxed in Eq.(3), aiming to reserve the intensity of user interests.\\n273: That is, normalization with softmax on the output of a⁡(⋅)a(\\\\cdot) is abandoned.\\n274: Instead, value of ∑iwi\\\\sum_{i}w_{i} is treated as an approximation of the intensity of activated user interests to some degree.\\n275: For example, if one user’s historical behaviors contain 90% clothes and 10% electronics.\\n276: Given two candidate ads of T-shirt and phone, T-shirt activates most of the historical behaviors belonging to clothes and may get larger value of 𝒗U\\\\bm{v}_{U} (higher intensity of interest) than phone.\\n277: Traditional attention methods lose the resolution on the numerical scale of 𝒗U\\\\bm{v}_{U} by normalizing of the output of a⁡(⋅)a(\\\\cdot).\\n278: We have tried LSTM to model user historical behavior data in the sequential manner.\\n279: But it shows no improvement.\\n280: Different from text which is under the constraint of grammar in NLP task, the sequence of user historical behaviors may contain multiple concurrent interests.\\n281: Rapid jumping and sudden ending over these interests causes the sequence data of user behaviors to seem to be noisy.\\n282: A possible direction is to design special structures to model such data in a sequence way.\\n283: We leave it for future research.\\n284: 5. Training Techniques\\n285: In the advertising system in Alibaba, numbers of goods and users scale up to hundreds of millions.\\n286: Practically, training industrial deep networks with large scale sparse input features is of great challenge.\\n287: In this section, we introduce two important techniques which are proven to be helpful in practice.\\n288: 5.1. Mini-batch Aware Regularization\\n289: Overfitting is a critical challenge for training industrial networks.\\n290: For example, with addition of fine-grained features, such as features of goods_ids with dimensionality of 0.6 billion (including v​i​s​i​t​e​d​_​g​o​o​d​s​_​i​d​svisited\\\\_goods\\\\_ids of user and g​o​o​d​s​_​i​dgoods\\\\_id of ad as described in Table 1), model performance falls rapidly after the first epoch during training without regularization, as the dark green line shown in Fig.4 in later section 6.5.\\n291: It is not practical to directly apply traditional regularization methods, such as ℓ2\\\\ell_{2} and ℓ1\\\\ell_{1} regularization, on training networks with sparse inputs and hundreds of millions of parameters.\\n292: Take ℓ2\\\\ell_{2} regularization as an example.\"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'python3 /workspace/tools.py read unirank-benchmark-v2 --find HiFormer --lines 24'","aggregated_output":"/bin/bash: warning: setlocale: LC_ALL: cannot change locale (C.UTF-8)\n/bin/bash: warning: setlocale: LC_ALL: cannot change locale (C.UTF-8)\n{\n  \"id\": \"unirank-benchmark-v2\",\n  \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/html/2607.19987v2\",\n  \"version\": \"arxiv:2607.19987v2\",\n  \"access_scope\": \"Cached section2.3 method descriptions; not an original-method full-paper audit.\",\n  \"text_sha256\": \"34ff9c412b35f3118aae377faca5f658d7bffac0b4ef1c12a4eb0c7937372789\",\n  \"total_lines\": 39,\n  \"start_line\": 7,\n  \"next_line\": 31,\n  \"truncated\": false,\n  \"text\": \"7: This paradigm arranges sequential modeling and feature interaction as consecutive modules. The sequence module first encodes the behavioral history, and the feature interaction module then combines this representation with user, target-item, and context features.\\n8: •\\n9: HiFormer (Gui et al., 2023) pools the behavior history with target attention, tokenizes heterogeneous fields and task queries, and applies hierarchical HiFormer blocks to the stacked tokens.\\n10: •\\n11: RankMixer (Zhu et al., 2025) pools the history with target attention, tokenizes concatenated ranking fields into chunks, and alternates parameter-free Token Mixing with per-token MLPs.\\n12: •\\n13: Zenith (Zhang et al., 2026) separately embeds user and item identifiers, pools historical item attributes with target attention, tokenizes context and target attributes, and applies Zenith interaction blocks.\\n14: •\\n15: TokenMixer (Jiang et al., 2026) combines target-aware sequence summaries with global and group tokens, then applies reversible token mixing and per-token feed-forward networks.\\n16: •\\n17: UniMixer (Ha et al., 2026) flattens histories with user and context fields, then chunk-tokenizes them for UniMixing-Lite blocks.\\n18: •\\n19: HeMix (Wang et al., 2026) combines fixed and feature-conditioned queries with low-rank token mixing over recent and earlier histories.\\n20: •\\n21: SSR (Yu et al., 2026) uses target-attention sequence pooling and multi-view competitive sparse filters with dense fusion.\\n22: 2.3.2. Layer-wise Unified Interaction\\n23: This paradigm integrates sequential and non-sequential representations throughout every network layer.\\n24: •\\n25: OneTrans (Zhang et al., 2025) injects sequence and partitioned non-sequential tokens into every block, using mixed attention, a shared sequence FFN, and token-specific FFNs.\\n26: •\\n27: HyFormer (Huang et al., 2026b) encodes the sequence per layer, decodes global queries, and enriches them with non-sequential tokens.\\n28: •\\n29: MixFormer (Huang et al., 2026a) mixes non-sequential queries, uses cross-attention to retrieve information from the updated sequence, and fuses the resulting representations.\\n30: •\"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'python3 /workspace/tools.py read unirank-code:HiFormer --start-line 140 --lines 100'","aggregated_output":"/bin/bash: warning: setlocale: LC_ALL: cannot change locale (C.UTF-8)\n/bin/bash: warning: setlocale: LC_ALL: cannot change locale (C.UTF-8)\n{\n  \"id\": \"unirank-code:HiFormer\",\n  \"url\": \"https://github.com/salmon1802/UniRank/blob/90bb99a60724e975ec565836266fd86dcad0039d/model_zoo/HiFormer.py\",\n  \"version\": \"git:90bb99a60724e975ec565836266fd86dcad0039d\",\n  \"access_scope\": \"Full pinned benchmark source for inspection; not proof of equivalence to the original author method.\",\n  \"text_sha256\": \"ff8b2e9cf9112ff50a807e1b85b8236ecaa699f842b93b6209630cace8b454a3\",\n  \"total_lines\": 239,\n  \"start_line\": 140,\n  \"next_line\": 240,\n  \"truncated\": false,\n  \"text\": \"140:         y_pred = [self.output_activation[i](tower_output[i]) for i in range(self.num_tasks)]\\n141:         return_dict = {}\\n142:         labels = self.feature_map.labels\\n143:         for i in range(self.num_tasks):\\n144:             return_dict[\\\"{}_pred\\\".format(labels[i])] = y_pred[i]\\n145:         return return_dict\\n146: \\n147: \\n148: class HiFormerTokenizer(nn.Module):\\n149:     def __init__(self, input_dim, token_dim, num_tokens, num_tasks):\\n150:         super(HiFormerTokenizer, self).__init__()\\n151:         self.task_tokens = nn.init.xavier_uniform_(\\n152:             nn.Parameter(torch.empty(1, num_tasks, token_dim))\\n153:         )\\n154:         self.token_mlps = FieldWiseTokenizer(input_dim, token_dim, num_tokens)\\n155: \\n156:     def forward(self, feature_embeddings):\\n157:         tokens = self.token_mlps(feature_embeddings)\\n158:         task_tokens = self.task_tokens.expand(tokens.size(0), -1, -1)\\n159:         return torch.cat([tokens, task_tokens], dim=1)\\n160: \\n161: \\n162: class HiFormerBlocks(nn.Module):\\n163:     def __init__(self,\\n164:                  token_dim,\\n165:                  num_token,\\n166:                  num_layers,\\n167:                  expand=2,\\n168:                  num_heads=2,\\n169:                  qkv_rank=256,\\n170:                  net_dropout=0.0):\\n171:         super(HiFormerBlocks, self).__init__()\\n172:         self.num_layers = num_layers\\n173:         self.mixer_norms = nn.ModuleList([\\n174:             nn.LayerNorm(token_dim)\\n175:             for _ in range(num_layers)\\n176:         ])\\n177:         self.pffn_norms = nn.ModuleList([\\n178:             nn.LayerNorm(token_dim)\\n179:             for _ in range(num_layers)\\n180:         ])\\n181: \\n182:         self.mixer_layers = nn.ModuleList([\\n183:             HiformerAttentionLayer(token_dim=token_dim,\\n184:                                         num_token=num_token,\\n185:                                         num_heads=num_heads,\\n186:                                         qkv_rank=qkv_rank)\\n187:             for _ in range(num_layers)\\n188:         ])\\n189:         self.pffn_layers = nn.ModuleList([\\n190:             PerTokenFeedForward(input_dim=token_dim,\\n191:                                 num_token=num_token,\\n192:                                 expand=expand,\\n193:                                 net_dropout=net_dropout)\\n194:             for _ in range(num_layers)\\n195:         ])\\n196: \\n197:     def forward(self, x: torch.Tensor):\\n198:         for i in range(self.num_layers):\\n199:             x = self.mixer_layers[i](self.mixer_norms[i](x)) + x\\n200:             x = self.pffn_layers[i](self.pffn_norms[i](x)) + x\\n201:         return x\\n202: \\n203: class LowRankLinear(nn.Module):\\n204:     def __init__(self, input_dim, output_dim, rank, bias=False):\\n205:         super(LowRankLinear, self).__init__()\\n206:         assert rank > 0, \\\"rank must be positive\\\"\\n207:         self.down_proj = nn.Linear(input_dim, rank, bias=False)\\n208:         self.up_proj = nn.Linear(rank, output_dim, bias=bias)\\n209: \\n210:     def forward(self, x):\\n211:         return self.up_proj(self.down_proj(x))\\n212: \\n213: \\n214: class HiformerAttentionLayer(nn.Module):\\n215:     def __init__(self,\\n216:                  token_dim,\\n217:                  num_token,\\n218:                  num_heads=2,\\n219:                  qkv_rank=256):\\n220:         super(HiformerAttentionLayer, self).__init__()\\n221:         assert token_dim % num_heads == 0, \\\"token_dim must be divisible by num_heads\\\"\\n222:         self.num_heads = num_heads\\n223:         self.head_dim = token_dim // num_heads\\n224:         self.token_dim = token_dim\\n225:         self.num_token = num_token\\n226:         input_dim = num_token * token_dim\\n227:         self.W_q = LowRankLinear(input_dim, input_dim, qkv_rank, bias=False)\\n228:         self.W_k = LowRankLinear(input_dim, input_dim, qkv_rank, bias=False)\\n229:         self.W_v = LowRankLinear(input_dim, input_dim, qkv_rank, bias=False)\\n230:         self.dot_attention = ScaledDotProductAttention()\\n231: \\n232:     def forward(self, x: torch.Tensor): # B × T × D\\n233:         x = x.flatten(start_dim=1) # B × TD\\n234:         Q = self.W_q(x).view(-1, self.num_token, self.num_heads, self.head_dim).transpose(1, 2) # [B, H, T, Dh]\\n235:         K = self.W_k(x).view(-1, self.num_token, self.num_heads, self.head_dim).transpose(1, 2)\\n236:         V = self.W_v(x).view(-1, self.num_token, self.num_heads, self.head_dim).transpose(1, 2)\\n237:         attn_out, _ = self.dot_attention(Q, K, V, scale=self.head_dim ** 0.5)\\n238:         attn_out = attn_out.transpose(1, 2).contiguous().view(-1, self.num_token, self.token_dim)  # [B, T, D]\\n239:         return attn_out\"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
